"""
On-off electrical consumer with start-up + cycle power signatures.

Ported from `ShipSIM.Components.Electrical.OnOffConsumer`.

The Modelica original extended `RandomStart`, which kept a `discrete Real NextStep`
state and used `when time >= pre(NextStep) then …` events seeded by a normal-noise RNG
to flip a `Work::BooleanOutput` between on and off cycles. With no Modelica `when`
events in Dyad, this port drops the random scheduler and takes the on/off state as an
external real-input `WorkSignal` (1 = on, 0 = off; intermediate values scale linearly).
The user can drive `WorkSignal` from `BlockComponents.Sources.Square` for a periodic
duty cycle, or from any random / scheduling source they implement themselves.

The two power-shape tables (`StartTable`, `CycleTable`) are replaced by a single
parameterized cycle that ramps to nominal power over `StartTime` seconds and then
oscillates around 1.0 at `CycleAmplitude` and `CycleFrequency`. Same conceptual purpose:
output = NominalPower · Kr · cycle_factor(time_since_on) when on, 0 when off.

Limitations vs upstream:
- No randomness in the on/off pattern.
- No optional `Integrators` sub-component (`getIntegrators = true`); the user can
  feed `y` into a `BlockComponents.Continuous.Integrator` externally.
- A precise replay of the upstream's table-based start signature would need the user
  to provide their own external time-shaped signal multiplied by `WorkSignal`.
"""
component OnOffConsumer
  parameter NominalPower::Real = 1000
  "Percentage of nominal power used [0..1]"
  parameter Kr::Real = 0.85
  "Start-up rise time [s] — the unit ramps from 0 to nominal over this period after `Work` flips on."
  parameter StartTime::Real = 5
  "Steady-cycle small-amplitude variation around nominal."
  parameter CycleAmplitude::Real = 0.1
  "Steady-cycle frequency [Hz]."
  parameter CycleFrequency::Real = 1.0

  WorkSignal = RealInput()
  y = RealOutput()

  variable time_on::Real      # tracks how long the consumer has been on
  variable shape::Real
relations
  initial time_on = 0

  # Time-since-on integrator: increments only when WorkSignal > 0.5; resets toward 0
  # when WorkSignal goes back below 0.5 (continuous approximation).
  der(time_on) = ifelse(WorkSignal > 0.5, 1, -time_on)

  # Shape: ramp to 1.0 over StartTime, then small steady oscillation.
  shape = ifelse(time_on < StartTime,
                 time_on / StartTime,
                 1 + CycleAmplitude * sin(2 * π * CycleFrequency * (time_on - StartTime)))

  y = WorkSignal * NominalPower * Kr * shape
end
